.TH LT-QWWAD_EF_SQUARE_WELL "1" "September 2016" "lt-qwwad_ef_square_well (Quantum Wells, Wires and Dots) 1.5.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lt-qwwad_ef_square_well \- compute eigenstates of a finite square quantum well
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lt-qwwad_ef_square_well
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Find the eigenstates of a single finite quantum well.
.SH OPTIONS

Generic options:
.TP
\fB\-h\fR [ \fB\-\-help\fR ]
display a help message
.TP
\fB\-v\fR [ \fB\-\-version\fR ]
display the version of the program
.HP
\fB\-c\fR [ \fB\-\-config\fR ] arg (=qwwad.cfg) name of the configuration file to be used
.SS "Program-specific options:"
.TP
\fB\-a\fR [ \fB\-\-wellwidth\fR ] arg (=100)
Width of quantum well [angstrom].
.TP
\fB\-b\fR [ \fB\-\-barrierwidth\fR ] arg (=200)
Width of barrier [angstrom]. Note that this
is only used for the purposes of outputting
the data. The calculation here assumes that
the barriers are infinitely thick.  As
such, the wavefunctions do not decay to
precisely zero at the boundaries.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR [ \fB\-\-wellmass\fR ] arg (=0.067)
Effective mass in well (relative to that of
a free electron)
.TP
\fB\-n\fR [ \fB\-\-barriermass\fR ] arg (=0.067) Effective mass in barrier (relative to that
of a free electron)
.TP
\fB\-\-outputequations\fR
Output the matching equations for the
system. The left\-hand side of the equation
is output to 'lhs.r' and each branch of the
right\-hand side is output to a set of
\&'rhs_i.r' files, where 'i' is the index of
the state that lies on that branch. An rhs
file is output for all the bound states in
the system and one additional branch (with
no real solution)
.TP
\fB\-\-outputpotential\fR
Output the potential profile for the system
to v.r
.TP
\fB\-p\fR [ \fB\-\-particle\fR ] arg (=e)
ID of particle to be used: 'e', 'h' or 'l',
for electrons, heavy holes or light holes
respectively.
.TP
\fB\-N\fR [ \fB\-\-nz\fR ] arg (=1000)
Number of spatial points for output file.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR [ \fB\-\-nst\fR ] arg (=1)
Number of states to find
.TP
\fB\-\-barrierpotential\fR arg (=100)
Barrier potential [meV]
.TP
\fB\-\-Ecutoff\fR arg
Cut\-off energy for solutions [meV]
.SS "Configuration options:"
.TP
\fB\-V\fR [ \fB\-\-verbose\fR ]
display lots of information about calculation
.SH FILES
.SS Output files:
  'E*.r'    Energy of each state:
            Column 1: state index.
            Column 2: energy [meV].
  'wf_*i.r' Wave function amplitude at each position
            Column 1: position [m].
            Column 2: wave function amplitude [m^{-1/2}].
  'v.r'     Potential profile (if --outputpotential flag is used)
            Column 1: position [m].
            Column 2: potential [J].
  'lhs.r'   Left-hand side of matching function (if --outputequations flag is used)
            Column 1: Normalised well wave-vector
            Column 2: Normalised barrier decay constant
  'rhs_i.r' Right-hand side of matching function for branch i (if --outputequations flag is used)
            Column 1: Normalised well wave-vector
            Column 2: Normalised barrier decay constant

In each case, the '*' is replaced by the particle ID and the 'i' is replaced by the number of the state.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compute the first three states in a 150-angstrom well with 100 meV confining potential:
   qwwad_ef_square_well --wellwidth 150 --barrierpotential 100 --nst 3

Compute the first three heavy-hole states in a 200-angstrom well, using effective mass = 0.62 m0:
   qwwad_ef_square_well --wellwidth 200 --wellmass 0.62 --particle h

Compute the ground state in a 200 angstrom well with 100 meV barriers, and dump plots of the matching equations to file:
   qwwad_ef_square_well --wellwidth 200 --barrierpotential 100 --outputequations"
